  "image_name": "1bd02a9ec5850606c80aac4d84483a76.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how plant immune receptors detect invading fungal and bacterial pathogens through their secreted effectors. At the top, two pathogen types are shown interacting with a plant host cell: a haustorium-forming fungus that penetrates the host surface and a bacterium using a pilus-like structure. Both deliver effectors into the plant cell, depicted as different shapes for fungal and bacterial molecules.\n\nThe main message is that plant NLR receptors can recognize these pathogen effectors in three related ways. In direct recognition, the NLR appears to bind the effector itself. In integrated decoy-mediated recognition, the effector is sensed through a decoy domain built into the NLR. In indirect recognition, the effector does not seem to contact the NLR directly; instead it modifies a host target or decoy protein, and the NLR detects that altered host molecule.\n\nArrows and dashed arrows indicate that both fungal and bacterial effectors may be perceived by more than one recognition strategy. Overall, the figure highlights that plants monitor either pathogen molecules directly or their effects on host proteins, using multiple surveillance mechanisms to trigger immunity against diverse microbes.",
